<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wewoka, Oklahoma</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wewoka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wewoka is a town in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, founded in 1849 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-seminoles">Black Seminoles</a> led by John Horse. It became the capital of the Seminole Nation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a> and remains the seat of Seminole County. Its name is said to mean barking water in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wewoka-oklahoma.jpg" alt="Wewoka, Oklahoma" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kennethaw88 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seminole_County_OK_Courthouse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Wewoka's history begins with the Black Seminole John Horse, also known as Gopher John. In the spring of 1849 he and a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-seminoles">Black Seminoles</a> founded a settlement near the modern town, seeking safety and autonomy from the Creek Nation. It stood at the falls of a small stream between the North and South Canadian Rivers, and the name is said to come from the rush of water, We-Wo-Ka, meaning Barking Water in the Mvskoke language. Other historians say it was named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osceola">Osceola</a>'s second wife. In the autumn of 1849 Horse and the chief Wild Cat led families into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> to escape slavers.</p><p>The Seminole Nation had sided with the Confederacy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, and many of its members fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>. After the war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> required a new treaty in 1866, which obliged the nation to emancipate its slaves and give <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> who stayed full citizenship. Elijah J. Brown led the refugees back from Kansas, built a house and a trading post, and a post office opened in 1867. Seminole Governor John Brown had a log house built as the Seminole capitol in 1877, and the Wewoka Trading Company, run by the brothers John F. and A. J. Brown, was formed in 1891.</p><p>The town was platted in 1897. Lots were at first to be sold only to American Indians, but the decision was overturned in 1902 and sales opened to white settlers. Wewoka became part of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> in 1907 and in 1908 won an election against Seminole to become the seat of Seminole County.</p><p>Oil was discovered two miles southeast of Wewoka in 1923, within the Greater Seminole Oil Field. The population grew from 1,520 in 1925 to over 20,000 by 1927, making it briefly the third-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>. As the oil declined, jobs and people left, and the population was 6,753 in 1950 and 6,300 in 1960.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Wewoka is the county seat of Seminole County and the capital of the Seminole Nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>. Its population was 3,133 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The house built by Andrew Jackson Brown and his wife Mannie Lou, the last remaining structure of the Nineteenth Seminole Republic, is listed on the National Register of Historic Places. The Seminole National Museum, opened in 1974, is housed in a building put up by the Works Progress Administration in 1934.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wewoka, Oklahoma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Second Seminole War of 1835 to 1842, led by Osceola and Micanopy, the Seminole fought the United States to a costly standstill, but most were removed to Indian Territory, where <b>Wewoka</b> became their capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/seminole-nation">Seminole Nation</a></li></ul></section>
